libwavpack: the assembly is on wherever the C compiler can assemble it

Outside MSVC, libwavpack's assembly is the .S files, and CMake compiles a .S file as ASM -- with the C compiler, which preprocesses it first -- a language this list file already enables. HAVE_ASM asked instead for an ASM-ATT assembler, a separate program such as GNU as that CMake gives the .s and .asm extensions, none of which libwavpack has on those systems. So the build required a tool it never ran: with Clang and LLD and no binutils beside them, the assembly was off, and with binutils present it was on for a reason that had nothing to do with how the files are assembled. HAVE_ASM now asks for the ASM compiler, and ASM-ATT is no longer looked for. Against 5.9.0.

diff --git a/CMakeLists.txt b/CMakeLists.txt
index 0b5d26d..269bf52 100644
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -44,11 +44,6 @@ if(MSVC)
   elseif(WavPack_CPU_ARM32 OR WavPack_CPU_ARM64)
     enable_language(ASM_MARMASM)
   endif()
-else()
-  check_language(ASM-ATT)
-  if(CMAKE_ASM-ATT_COMPILER)
-    enable_language(ASM-ATT)
-  endif()
 endif()
 
 
@@ -131,7 +126,7 @@ if(WIN32 AND NOT MINGW)
         set(HAVE_MASM 1)
     endif()
 else()
-    if(CMAKE_ASM-ATT_COMPILER)
+    if(CMAKE_ASM_COMPILER)
         if(WavPack_CPU_X86 OR WavPack_CPU_X64 OR WavPack_CPU_ARM32)
             set(HAVE_ASM 1)
         endif()
